              "text": "Let me analyze the code to understand what needs to be tested.\n\nThe code is from `youtube_dl/downloader/dash.py`, specifically the `DashSegmentsFD.real_download` method. Lines 19-80 need coverage.\n\nLet me first get info about the relevant classes and methods to understand the structure.\n\nKey methods used:\n- `self.params.get('test', False)` - params dict\n- `self._prepare_and_start_frag_download(ctx)`\n- `self._download_fragment(ctx, fragment_url, info_dict)` - returns (success, frag_content)\n- `self._append_fragment(ctx, frag_content)`\n- `self.report_retry_fragment(err, frag_index, count, fragment_retries)`\n- `self.report_skip_fragment(frag_index)`\n- `self.report_error(...)`\n- `self._finish_frag_download(ctx)`\n\nLet me get info on these methods to understand their signatures and behavior.\n\nBranches to cover:\n1. Line 20-21: test mode (fragments[:1]) vs normal mode\n2. Line 36-37: frag_index <= ctx['fragment_index'] \u2192 continue (resume case)\n3. Line 40: fatal = i == 0 or not skip_unavailable_fragments\n4. Line 44-47: fragment_url from fragment.get('url') or urljoin with fragment_base_url and fragment['path']\n5. Line 49-50: not success \u2192 return False\n6. Line 52: break after successful append\n7. Line 53-62: HTTPError retry path\n8. Line 63-69: DownloadError path - skip if not fatal, raise if fatal\n9. Line 71-76: count > fragment_retries - skip if not fatal (continue), error if fatal (return False)\n10. Line 78-80: finish and return True\n\nLet me get info about the FragmentFD class and its methods first.\n\nI should call get_info on:\n- `DashSegmentsFD`\n- `FragmentFD`\n- `FragmentFD._download_fragment`\n- `FragmentFD._append_fragment`\n- `FragmentFD._prepare_and_start_frag_download`\n- `FragmentFD._finish_frag_download`\n- `FragmentFD.report_retry_fragment`\n- `FragmentFD.report_skip_fragment`\n- `FragmentFD.report_error`\n\nLet me make these calls.\n\nThen I'll write tests. The approach: subclass DashSegmentsFD or mock the fragment methods to control behavior.\n\nActually, a cleaner approach: create a test subclass that overrides `_download_fragment`, `_append_fragment`, `_prepare_and_start_frag_download`, `_finish_frag_download`, `report_retry_fragment`, `report_skip_fragment`, `report_error` to record calls and simulate errors.\n\nTest cases:\n1. **test_download_success**: normal download, all fragments succeed, returns True, _finish_frag_download called.\n2. **test_download_test_mode**: params test=True, only first fragment downloaded.\n3. **test_resume_skip**: ctx['fragment_index'] set (via _prepare_and_start_frag_download setting it), first fragment skipped.\n4. **test_urljoin_path**: fragment without 'url' but with 'path' and fragment_base_url set.\n5. **test_no_url_no_base**: fragment without url and no fragment_base_url \u2192 assert fails (AssertionError). Hmm, that's an assert statement; would raise AssertionError. Maybe test that it raises AssertionError. Actually that's a branch (line 45-46). To cover line 46-47 we need fragment without url but with base url. To cover the assert failing... we could test that AssertionError is raised. But careful: pytest.raises(AssertionError).\n6. **test_download_failure_returns_false**: _download_fragment returns (False, None) \u2192 return False.\n7. **test_http_error_retry_success**: HTTPError raised first time, then success \u2192 report_retry_fragment called.\n8. **test_http_error_give_up_fatal**: HTTPError every time, fatal (first fragment) \u2192 count > fragment_retries \u2192 report_error, return False.\n9. **test_http_error_give_up_skip**: HTTPError every time, not fatal (skip_unavailable_fragments=True, i>0) \u2192 report_skip_fragment, continue, return True.\n10. **test_download_error_skip**: DownloadError on non-first fragment with skip_unavailable_fragments=True \u2192 report_skip_fragment, break, continue, return True.\n11. **test_download_error_fatal_raise**: DownloadError on first fragment \u2192 raise DownloadError.\n\nAlso need to cover line 40's branches: fatal = i == 0 or not skip_unavailable_fragments. With skip_unavailable_fragments=False, fatal is True even for i>0. Test: DownloadError on second fragment with skip_unavailable_fragments=False \u2192 raise.\n\nLet me check how ctx['fragment_index'] is set. In FragmentFD._prepare_and_start_frag_download, ctx gets 'fragment_index' set based on resume. Let me get info.\n\nLet me call get_info for the relevant symbols.\n\nActually, the get_info tool takes a name like 'C.foo'. Let me query:\n- 'DashSegmentsFD'\n- 'FragmentFD'\n- 'FragmentFD._prepare_and_start_frag_download'\n- 'FragmentFD._download_fragment'\n- 'FragmentFD._append_fragment'\n- 'FragmentFD._finish_frag_download'\n- 'FragmentFD.report_retry_fragment'\n- 'FragmentFD.report_skip_fragment'\n- 'FragmentFD.report_error'\n- 'FragmentFD._prepare_url'\n\nLet me make these calls in parallel.\n\nThen write the test file.\n\nThe test file structure:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\nfrom youtube_dl.downloader.dash import DashSegmentsFD\nfrom youtube_dl.compat import compat_urllib_error\nfrom youtube_dl.utils import DownloadError\n\n\nclass FakeDashFD(DashSegmentsFD):\n    \"\"\"Test double that records calls and simulates configurable behavior.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, params=None, download_results=None, http_errors=0, download_errors=0):\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, DashSegmentsFD.__init__ \u2014 FragmentFD inherits from FileDownloader. FileDownloader.__init__(ydl, params). Let me check. FileDownloader takes (ydl, params). So we can instantiate with a fake ydl.\n\nActually simpler: instantiate DashSegmentsFD with a fake ydl object and params dict, then monkeypatch the fragment methods on the instance.\n\nLet me check FileDownloader.__init__ signature via get_info.\n\nLet me plan the fake ydl: needs to have... FileDownloader.__init__ sets self.ydl = ydl, self.params = params, self._progress_hooks = [], etc. It may call to_screen etc. only when reporting. report_error calls self.ydl.report_error? Let me check: FileDownloader.report_error calls self.ydl.report_error if self.ydl else to_stderr. Actually in youtube_dl, FileDownloader.report_error:\n\n```python\ndef report_error(self, msg, tb=None, expected=False):\n    self.ydl.report_error(msg, tb, expected)\n```\n\nHmm, but we're overriding report_error in our test double anyway? No wait \u2014 report_error is called on self (the FD), i.e., self.report_error. If we don't override it, it calls self.ydl.report_error. We can use a fake ydl that records errors. But to be safe and to assert, we can monkeypatch instance methods.\n\nSimplest approach: create instance, then set instance attributes to record calls:\n\n```python\nfd = DashSegmentsFD(ydl, params)\nfd._prepare_and_start_frag_download = lambda ctx: ctx.setdefault('fragment_index', 0)\n```\n\nWait, but real _prepare_and_start_frag_download sets ctx['fragment_index'] = 0 (or resume index). If we replace it, we control fragment_index for resume tests.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 for coverage of dash.py lines, we want to execute the real code in real_download only; the helper methods (_prepare_and_start_frag_download, _download_fragment, etc.) are defined in fragment.py (FragmentFD), not dash.py, so replacing them doesn't reduce dash.py coverage. Good.\n\nBut the instructions say \"Create new pytest test functions that execute all missing lines and branches\" \u2014 lines 19-80 of dash.py. So mocking the inherited helpers is fine.\n\nHowever, maybe better to use monkeypatch to patch methods on the class within test scope, to ensure cleanup. Using instance attribute assignment also gets cleaned up when instance is garbage collected. But monkeypatch is cleaner per instructions.\n\nLet me design:\n\n```python\nclass Recorder:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.calls = []\n```\n\nPlan per test:\n\n```python\ndef make_fd(monkeypatch, params=None, *, download_results=None, http_error_on=None, download_error_on=None):\n    ydl = FakeYDL()\n    fd = DashSegmentsFD(ydl, params or {})\n    ...\n```\n\nActually, let me look at how youtube_dl's own tests do it. In youtube_dl there's no test for dash downloader I think. But there's a testutils. Let me just write a fake ydl:\n\n```python\nclass FakeYDL:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.params = {}\n        self.reported_errors = []\n    def report_error(self, msg, tb=None, expected=False):\n        self.reported_errors.append(msg)\n        raise Exception('reported error')  # real ydl raises DownloadError\n```\n\nHmm, real YDL.report_error raises DownloadError. But in our test, we override fd.report_error anyway to record and control. Actually wait \u2014 line 75 calls self.report_error(...) then line 76 returns False. If real report_error raises, return False wouldn't be reached. In the real code, self.report_error \u2192 FileDownloader.report_error \u2192 self.ydl.report_error \u2192 raises DownloadError... Actually let me check FileDownloader.report_error in youtube_dl:\n\n```python\ndef report_error(self, msg, tb=None, expected=False):\n    self.ydl.report_error(msg, tb, expected)\n```\n\nAnd YoutubeDL.report_error raises DownloadError. Hmm, but then line 76 `return False` would never execute in production... unless ydl is None. Actually in FileDownloader.trouble:\n\n```python\ndef trouble(self, message, tb=None, expected=False):\n    if self.ydl is not None:\n        self.ydl.trouble(message, tb, expected)\n```\n\nAnd report_error... let me check. In youtube_dl/downloader/common.py:\n\n```python\ndef report_error(self, msg, tb=None, expected=False):\n    self.ydl.report_error(msg, tb, expected)\n```\n\nHmm, I need to check. Let me use get_info on 'FileDownloader.report_error' and 'FileDownloader.__init__'.\n\nAnyway, for the test, I'll monkeypatch fd.report_error to record the message and not raise, so line 76 (return False) executes.\n\nNow, the methods to patch per test:\n\n- `_prepare_and_start_frag_download(ctx)`: record call; set ctx['fragment_index'] = resume_index (default 0).\n- `_download_fragment(ctx, url, info_dict)`: record url; behavior based on configured script; return (True, b'data') or (False, None) or raise HTTPError/DownloadError.\n- `_append_fragment(ctx, content)`: record content.\n- `_finish_frag_download(ctx)`: record call.\n- `report_retry_fragment(err, frag_index, count, fragment_retries)`: record.\n- `report_skip_fragment(frag_index)`: record.\n- `report_error(msg)`: record.\n\nFor HTTPError: compat_urllib_error.HTTPError(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp). Construct with compat_urllib_request or just compat_urllib_error.HTTPError('http://x', 404, 'not found', None, None). That works.\n\nFor DownloadError: DownloadError('msg') \u2014 it's from youtube_dl.utils, subclass of YoutubeDLError. Its __init__ takes msg.\n\nNow the download behavior script: a function or a list of callables. Let me design a flexible fake:\n\n```python\nclass FragmentDownloader:\n    def __init__(self, behavior):\n        self.behavior = behavior  # dict: url fragment index -> list of outcomes\n        self.calls = []\n    def __call__(self, ctx, url, info_dict):\n        self.calls.append(url)\n        outcomes = self.behavior.get(url, [(True, b'data')])\n        outcome = outcomes.pop(0) if len(outcomes) > 1 else outcomes[0]\n        ...\n```\n\nSimpler: use a queue per URL.\n\n```python\ndef _download_fragment(self, ctx, url, info_dict):\n    outcomes = self._outcomes[url]\n    if len(outcomes) > 1:\n        outcome = outcomes.pop(0)\n    else:\n        outcome = outcomes[0]\n    kind, value = outcome\n    if kind == 'ok':\n        return True, value\n    if kind == 'fail':\n        return False, None\n    if kind == 'http_error':\n        raise compat_urllib_error.HTTPError(url, 404, 'Not Found', None, None)\n    if kind == 'download_error':\n        raise DownloadError(value)\n```\n\nNow let me enumerate tests to cover all lines/branches:\n\nLines:\n- 19: fragment_base_url = info_dict.get('fragment_base_url') \u2014 any test.\n- 20-21: both branches: test=True (fragments[:1]) and test=False.\n- 23-25: ctx creation \u2014 any test.\n- 28: _prepare_and_start_frag_download \u2014 any test.\n- 30-31: params.get fragment_retries, skip_unavailable_fragments \u2014 cover defaults and custom values.\n- 33-35: loop.\n- 36-37: continue when frag_index <= ctx['fragment_index'] \u2014 resume test with fragment_index=1.\n- 40: fatal computation \u2014 both i==0 and i>0 paths; also skip_unavailable_fragments=False.\n- 41-42: while loop.\n- 43-44: try, fragment_url = fragment.get('url').\n- 45-46: not fragment_url \u2192 assert fragment_base_url. Cover both: url present (skip 46-47) and url absent with base_url (execute 47). Also assert failing case (no base url) \u2192 AssertionError. Hmm, is that a branch? Line 46 `assert fragment_base_url` \u2014 if it fails, AssertionError propagates out of real_download (not caught by except clauses since AssertionError isn't HTTPError/DownloadError). Test with pytest.raises(AssertionError).\n- 47: urljoin.\n- 48: _download_fragment call.\n- 49-50: not success \u2192 return False.\n- 51: _append_fragment.\n- 52: break.\n- 53-60: HTTPError handler: count += 1; if count <= fragment_retries: report_retry_fragment.\n- 61-62: report_retry_fragment call (need count <= fragment_retries).\n- 63-66: DownloadError handler: if not fatal.\n- 67: report_skip_fragment.\n- 68: break.\n- 69: raise (fatal DownloadError).\n- 71-72: count > fragment_retries; if not fatal.\n- 73: report_skip_fragment.\n- 74: continue.\n- 75: report_error.\n- 76: return False.\n- 78: _finish_frag_download.\n- 80: return True.\n\nBranch coverage:\n- 20-21: test param True/False.\n- 36: True/False.\n- 40: fatal True (i==0), fatal True (skip=False, i>0), fatal False (skip=True, i>0).\n- 45: url present/absent.\n- 49: success True/False.\n- 53: HTTPError raised/not.\n- 61: count <= retries True/False.\n- 63: DownloadError raised/not.\n- 66: not fatal True/False.\n- 71: count > retries True/False.\n- 72: not fatal True/False.\n\nTest list:\n\n1. `test_success_all_fragments`: 2 fragments with 'url', params default. Assert returns True, finish called, appended contents, urls called in order. Covers 19-28, 30-35, 40 (fatal True for i=0, fatal False for i=1), 43-52, 78, 80.\n\n2. `test_test_mode_only_first_fragment`: params {'test': True}, 2 fragments. Assert only 1 download call. Covers 20-21 True branch.\n\n3. `test_resume_skips_downloaded_fragments`: monkeypatch _prepare_and_start_frag_download to set ctx['fragment_index'] = 1. 2 fragments. Assert only second fragment downloaded. Covers 36-37.\n\n4. `test_url_from_path_urljoin`: fragment without 'url', with 'path', fragment_base_url set. Assert downloaded url == urljoin(base, path). Covers 45-47.\n\n5. `test_missing_base_url_asserts`: fragment without 'url', no fragment_base_url. pytest.raises(AssertionError). Covers 46 assert failing.\n\n6. `test_download_fragment_failure_returns_false`: _download_fragment returns (False, None) for first fragment. Assert returns False, finish NOT called. Covers 49-50.\n\n7. `test_http_error_retried_then_success`: first fragment: http_error then ok. params fragment_retries=2. Assert returns True, report_retry_fragment called once with (frag_index=1, count=1, retries=2). Covers 53-62.\n\n8. `test_http_error_gives_up_fatal`: first fragment: http_error always, fragment_retries=1. fatal=True (i==0). Expect report_error called, returns False, finish not called. Covers 61 False branch, 71-76.\n\n9. `test_http_error_gives_up_skip`: second fragment (i=1) http_error always, skip_unavailable_fragments=True (default), fragment_retries=0. Expect report_skip_fragment, returns True, finish called. Covers 71-74.\n\nWait, with fragment_retries=0: while count <= 0: try \u2192 http_error \u2192 count=1; if count <= 0 \u2192 False, no report_retry. Loop: count(1) > 0 \u2192 not fatal \u2192 report_skip_fragment, continue. Good. Covers 61-62 False branch and 71-74.\n\n10. `test_download_error_skipped_nonfatal`: second fragment raises DownloadError, skip default True. Expect report_skip_fragment called, returns True. Covers 63-68.\n\n11. `test_download_error_fatal_first_fragment_raises`: first fragment raises DownloadError. Expect pytest.raises(DownloadError). Covers 69.\n\n12. `test_download_error_fatal_skip_disabled`: second fragment raises DownloadError with skip_unavailable_fragments=False \u2192 fatal=True \u2192 raises. Covers 40 with skip=False and 69.\n\n13. `test_http_error_fatal_skip_disabled`: maybe not needed; 8 covers fatal http give-up.\n\nAlso cover line 30-31 with explicit params: fragment_retries and skip_unavailable_fragments passed in params \u2014 covered by tests 7, 8, 12.\n\nAlso, for line 40 fatal = i == 0 or not skip_unavailable_fragments \u2014 need both operands exercised: i==0 True (tests 8, 11), i>0 & skip True \u2192 False (tests 9, 10), i>0 & skip False \u2192 True (test 12). Good.\n\nNow, about the ctx['fragment_index']: real _prepare_and_start_frag_download sets it. Since we patch it, we set it ourselves. In test 1 etc., set ctx['fragment_index'] = 0.\n\nWait \u2014 actually, should I patch these methods or use the real ones? The real `_prepare_and_start_frag_download` requires a lot of setup (opens file, temp files...). Patching is much cleaner. The instructions say to use monkeypatch where appropriate \u2014 yes.\n\nImplementation detail: monkeypatch.setattr on the instance: `monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_download_fragment', fake)` \u2014 works since monkeypatch.setattr accepts objects with attribute names.\n\nLet me now write the fake ydl. FileDownloader.__init__(self, ydl, params):\n\n```python\ndef __init__(self, ydl, params):\n    self._ydl = ydl\n    self.params = params\n    self._progress_hooks = []\n    self._progress_visible = False\n```\n\nHmm, I don't remember exactly. Let me get_info on FileDownloader.__init__ to be sure. Also check whether params needs to be a dict \u2014 yes.\n\nFakeYDL minimal:\n\n```python\nclass FakeYDL:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.params = {}\n```\n\nFileDownloader.__init__ might reference ydl.params? Let me check via get_info.\n\nLet me also check DashSegmentsFD and FragmentFD to confirm method names: `_download_fragment`, `_append_fragment`, `_prepare_and_start_frag_download`, `_finish_frag_download`, `report_retry_fragment`, `report_skip_fragment`, `report_error`, and `to_screen`.\n\nLet me make the get_info calls now. I'll batch independent calls:\n\n- 'DashSegmentsFD'\n- 'FragmentFD'\n- 'FragmentFD._prepare_and_start_frag_download'\n- 'FragmentFD._download_fragment'\n- 'FragmentFD._append_fragment'\n- 'FragmentFD._finish_frag_download'\n- 'FragmentFD.report_retry_fragment'\n- 'FragmentFD.report_skip_fragment'\n- 'FileDownloader.__init__'\n- 'FileDownloader.report_error'\n\nThat's a lot; the tool presumably returns info about symbols in the codebase. Let me do it.\n\nAfter that, write the test file.\n\nLet me draft the test file:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Tests for youtube_dl.downloader.dash.DashSegmentsFD.real_download.\"\"\"\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom youtube_dl.compat import compat_urllib_error\nfrom youtube_dl.downloader.dash import DashSegmentsFD\nfrom youtube_dl.utils import DownloadError, urljoin\n\n\nclass FakeYDL(object):\n    \"\"\"Minimal stand-in for YoutubeDL.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.params = {}\n        self.errors = []\n\n\ndef http_error(url):\n    return compat_urllib_error.HTTPError(url, 404, 'Not Found', None, None)\n\n\nclass OutcomeRunner(object):\n    \"\"\"Callable for _download_fragment that replays scripted outcomes per URL.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, script):\n        # script: {url: [outcome, ...]}; outcome is ('ok', bytes) | ('fail', None)\n        # | ('http_error', None) | ('download_error', msg). The last outcome repeats.\n        self.script = dict(script)\n        self.urls = []\n\n    def __call__(self, ctx, url, info_dict):\n        self.urls.append(url)\n        outcomes = self.script.setdefault(url, [('ok', b'fragment-data')])\n        outcome = outcomes.pop(0) if len(outcomes) > 1 else outcomes[0]\n        kind, value = outcome\n        if kind == 'ok':\n            return True, value\n        if kind == 'fail':\n            return False, None\n        if kind == 'http_error':\n            raise http_error(url)\n        if kind == 'download_error':\n            raise DownloadError(value)\n        raise AssertionError('unknown outcome %r' % (kind,))\n\n\ndef make_fd(monkeypatch, params=None, script=None, resume_from=0):\n    \"\"\"Build a DashSegmentsFD with its fragment helpers stubbed out.\"\"\"\n    ydl = FakeYDL()\n    fd = DashSegmentsFD(ydl, params or {})\n\n    runner = OutcomeRunner(script or {})\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_download_fragment', runner)\n\n    calls = {'prepare': [], 'append': [], 'finish': [], 'retry': [], 'skip': [], 'error': []}\n\n    def prepare(ctx):\n        calls['prepare'].append(dict(ctx))\n        ctx['fragment_index'] = resume_from\n\n    def append(ctx, content):\n        calls['append'].append(content)\n\n    def finish(ctx):\n        calls['finish'].append(dict(ctx))\n\n    def retry(err, frag_index, count, fragment_retries):\n        calls['retry'].append((frag_index, count, fragment_retries))\n\n    def skip(frag_index):\n        calls['skip'].append(frag_index)\n\n    def error(msg, tb=None, expected=False):\n        calls['error'].append(msg)\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_prepare_and_start_frag_download', prepare)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_append_fragment', append)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_finish_frag_download', finish)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, 'report_retry_fragment', retry)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, 'report_skip_fragment', skip)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, 'report_error', error)\n\n    return fd, runner, calls\n\n\ndef frag(url):\n    return {'url': url}\n```\n\nWait \u2014 report_error signature: FileDownloader.report_error(self, msg, tb=None, expected=False)? Let me check with get_info. In youtube_dl/downloader/common.py:\n\n```python\ndef report_error(self, msg, tb=None, expected=False):\n    self.ydl.report_error(msg, tb, expected)\n```\n\nHmm, actually I recall in common.py:\n\n```python\ndef report_error(self, msg, tb=None, expected=False):\n    self.ydl.report_error(msg, tb, expected)\n```\n\nI'll verify with get_info. Similarly report_retry_fragment(self, err, frag_index, count, fragment_retries) and report_skip_fragment(self, frag_index) in fragment.py.\n\nAlso `_prepare_and_start_frag_download(self, ctx)` in fragment.py.\n\nAlso need to double check `DashSegmentsFD.__init__` \u2014 it's FragmentFD's, which is FileDownloader's: `def __init__(self, ydl, params)`. Verify.\n\nAlso `real_download(self, filename, info_dict)`.\n\nNow the tests:\n\n```python\nFRAGMENTS = [\n    {'url': 'http://example.com/seg-1.m4s'},\n    {'url': 'http://example.com/seg-2.m4s'},\n]\n\n\ndef test_success_downloads_all_fragments(monkeypatch):\n    fd, runner, calls = make_fd(monkeypatch)\n    info = {'fragments': list(FRAGMENTS)}\n\n    result = fd.real_download('dest.mp4', info)\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert runner.urls == ['http://example.com/seg-1.m4s', 'http://example.com/seg-2.m4s']\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'fragment-data', b'fragment-data']\n    assert len(calls['prepare']) == 1\n    assert len(calls['finish']) == 1\n    assert calls['skip'] == []\n    assert calls['error'] == []\n    assert calls['retry'] == []\n```\n\nCovers: 19, 20-21 (False branch), 23-28, 30-31 defaults, 33-35, 40 (both i==0 fatal and i>0 non-fatal), 42-52, 78, 80.\n\n```python\ndef test_test_mode_downloads_only_first_fragment(monkeypatch):\n    fd, runner, calls = make_fd(monkeypatch, params={'test': True})\n    info = {'fragments': list(FRAGMENTS)}\n\n    result = fd.real_download('dest.mp4', info)\n\n    assert result is True\n    assert runner.urls == ['http://example.com/seg-1.m4s']\n    assert calls['append'] == [b'fragment-data']\n    assert len(cal